  "prompt_text": "The figure is a schematic of an integrated laser device built on a layered chip. A pump laser enters from the left and is first amplified, then guided through a long spiral waveguide on the chip surface. As the pump light circulates through this spiral, it gradually dissipates, suggesting that the extended path is used to absorb or spread the pump energy efficiently before it reaches the active cavity.\n\nNear the end of the spiral, the light is directed into a small microring resonator, which appears to be the element that generates the laser output shown leaving to the right. A nearby heating element is placed beside the waveguide and microring, implying thermal tuning or control of the resonator. The materials are labeled as titanium-doped sapphire on top of sapphire and silicon dioxide, indicating that the active laser medium is integrated into a compact photonic platform rather than being a bulk crystal.\n\nOverall, the image conveys how pump light is routed, absorbed, and coupled into a microring resonator to produce laser emission on a chip-scale device.",
